From cdacf0e8a1a4a45e4de76d043164bb1546b85288 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 22:26:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: measure full paced period for RUNMANIFEST percentiles,
 not work time

Patch 0034's percentile capture read `frameTime` -- the pacer's own
per-frame WORK-time measurement, deliberately excluding that frame's own
sleep so the extraTime math a few lines below stays correct. Feeding the
same quantity into the RUNMANIFEST fps_p50/fps_p95 buffer was wrong: work
time is structurally >= lockedFrameRate whenever the pacer has slack to
spend (this rig always does in steady state, ~24ms/frame per
docs/BENCHMARK-PLAN.md), so 1/work-time cannot fall below 15fps no matter
how the frame actually paced out. The first real-hardware run against
0034 confirmed this on the nose: fps_p50=fps_p95=16.67 (60ms of work,
well inside the documented 42-58ms range), not a stall, not clock noise
-- just the wrong quantity for a percentile meant to catch pacer stalls.

Fix: capture the full paced period instead -- the delta between
consecutive `lastFrameTimeStamp` values, both taken AFTER that frame's
own sleep (i.e. between two "start timing next frame" timestamps). In
steady state this sits at ~1/lockedFrameRate (~66.67ms), because the
pacer pads work time up to that budget every frame, and only grows past
it on a genuine stall -- the actual "5% of frames were slower" shape a
stall-catching p95 needs.

Mechanically: the old capture site (reading `frameTime`, right after it
is computed) is removed outright; `frameTime` itself and the extraTime
math that consumes it are untouched -- this patch does not touch the
pacer. The capture moves to the "start timing next frame" reassignment
at the bottom of the loop: take one fresh timestamp, use it both to
compute this frame's paced period (against the OLD lastFrameTimeStamp)
and, after that, as the new lastFrameTimeStamp -- same single clock read
the original code always made at that point, so this adds no extra
Time::getCurrentTime() call. The existing reject-filter
(non-positive / below DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S) is reused
verbatim against the new quantity; a real paced period should never come
close to that 1ms floor, but it stays as a defensive sanity check.

Names (frameTimeSamples/frameTimeSampleCount/frameTimeRejected*/
DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S/dosPortCompareFrameTimes) are kept
as-is -- renaming every call site for a quantity that is still, in a
real sense, "the frame time RUNMANIFEST cares about" bought less clarity
than it cost in diff noise. Comments at both the removed and new capture
sites, and the DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S definition, are
rewritten so none of them still describe the old (work-time) semantics.

The percentile computation itself (sort, p50Index/p95Index, the
1.0/sorted[...] inversion, binary_sha12 embedding, RUNMANIFEST emit) is
unchanged -- only the input reaching that buffer changes.

Kept as a separate patch rather than amended into 0034: a real-hardware
PASS result (docs/benchmarks/cirrus-cl-gd5434-2026-09-02.md,
build_sha12=9c90db0e7905) already cites 0034's own diff, so that file
stays frozen.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TYcLv9sr97HFUcYBXKMgTu
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 70 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 50 insertions(+), 20 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index 270d5cd..0f7dcd9 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -63,8 +63,12 @@ int main( int inArgCount, char **inArgs ) {
 // somehow exceeds this.
 #define DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_SAMPLE_CAP 20000
 
-// DOS-PORT: floor below which a captured frameTime cannot be a real
-// measurement, derived from the clock's own documented granularity
+// DOS-PORT: floor below which a captured sample -- the full paced period
+// between successive frames (see playGame()'s exit-block capture site;
+// this used to be the per-frame work time `frameTime`, before patch 0035
+// moved the capture site to also include each frame's own pacer sleep) --
+// cannot be a real measurement, derived from the clock's own documented
+// granularity
 // (Time::getCurrentTime() truncates to whole milliseconds -- TimeDOS.cpp's
 // tv_usec/1000, see the pacer's own comment on this above), not from any
 // assumption about how fast this game's per-frame work can get. A real
@@ -1613,24 +1617,16 @@ char playGame() {
         
         double frameTime = newTimestamp - lastFrameTimeStamp;
 
-#ifdef __DJGPP__
-        // DOS-PORT: capture the real per-frame duration for the
-        // RUNMANIFEST percentile computation, before any pacing/sleep
-        // below adjusts anything -- see this function's exit block.
-        // Reject implausible readings rather than trusting the clock
-        // blindly -- see DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S's own comment.
-        if( frameTime <= 0.0 ) {
-            frameTimeRejectedNonPositive ++;
-            }
-        else if( frameTime < DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S ) {
-            frameTimeRejectedTooSmall ++;
-            }
-        else if( frameTimeSampleCount < DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_SAMPLE_CAP ) {
-            frameTimeSamples[ frameTimeSampleCount ] = frameTime;
-            frameTimeSampleCount ++;
-            }
-#endif
-
+        // DOS-PORT: `frameTime` is pure work time (render + game logic +
+        // audio pump), deliberately excluding this frame's own pacer
+        // sleep below -- that is what the extraTime math immediately
+        // below needs. It is NOT captured into the RUNMANIFEST percentile
+        // buffer here any more (patch 0034 did; patch 0035 moved it) --
+        // work time is structurally >= lockedFrameRate whenever the pacer
+        // has slack to spend, which is every steady-state frame on this
+        // rig, so it cannot show a pacer stall. See the capture site at
+        // this function's "start timing next frame" reassignment below,
+        // which measures the full paced period instead.
         double extraTime = 1.0 / lockedFrameRate - frameTime;
         
         // DOS-PORT: SDL_Delay, not Thread::staticSleep -- on DOS,
@@ -1858,8 +1854,42 @@ char playGame() {
             }
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+        // DOS-PORT: capture the full paced period -- the delta between
+        // this and the PREVIOUS "start timing next frame" timestamp, both
+        // taken AFTER their own pacer sleep -- for the RUNMANIFEST
+        // percentile buffer. Patch 0034 captured `frameTime` (pure work
+        // time, above) here instead; that quantity excludes every frame's
+        // own sleep by construction, so it is structurally >= 15fps
+        // whenever the pacer has any slack to spend (this rig always
+        // does in steady state) and cannot expose a pacer stall, which is
+        // the whole point of a stall-catching p95. The paced period sits
+        // at ~1/lockedFrameRate (~66.67ms) in steady state -- the pacer
+        // pads work time up to that budget every frame -- and only grows
+        // past it on a genuine stall, which is the actual "5% of frames
+        // were slower" shape p95 needs. Same reject-filter as before,
+        // applied to this quantity now instead of `frameTime` -- see
+        // DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S's own comment.
+        double pacedPeriodTimestamp = Time::getCurrentTime();
+        double pacedPeriod = pacedPeriodTimestamp - lastFrameTimeStamp;
+
+        if( pacedPeriod <= 0.0 ) {
+            frameTimeRejectedNonPositive ++;
+            }
+        else if( pacedPeriod < DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_MIN_PLAUSIBLE_S ) {
+            frameTimeRejectedTooSmall ++;
+            }
+        else if( frameTimeSampleCount < DOS_PORT_FRAME_TIME_SAMPLE_CAP ) {
+            frameTimeSamples[ frameTimeSampleCount ] = pacedPeriod;
+            frameTimeSampleCount ++;
+            }
+
+        // start timing next frame
+        lastFrameTimeStamp = pacedPeriodTimestamp;
+#else
         // start timing next frame
         lastFrameTimeStamp = Time::getCurrentTime();
+#endif
 
 
         // DOS-PORT: rest-of-loop-cost timing (was here through patch
